INT. BACK ROOM, FISH MARKET - PRE-DAWN

Crushed ice. Crates of mackerel. A bare bulb swings overhead.

SALVATORE "SAL THE KNIFE" BIANCHINI (50s, rubber apron, scarred knuckles) counts cash into a fish crate. His nephew, LORENZO "RENZO" BIANCHINI (20s, nervous, wet cuffs), holds the lid.

At a steel table, DETECTIVE MARISELA OYELOWO-KANE (40s, trench coat, notebook) sits perfectly still. Across from her, the bookkeeper, GRETCHEN VOLKOV (50s, cardigan, reading glasses on a cord), sorts receipts.

By the freezer door, a hulking enforcer called DOMINGO "THE HAMMER" TAVARES (30s, tailored suit under a slicker) watches the others.

SAL
Renzo, keep your hands where I see them.

RENZO
They're on the lid, Uncle Sal.

SAL
Then keep them there.

GRETCHEN
(not looking up)
Your numbers don't add, Salvatore.

SAL
Gretchen, my numbers always add.

GRETCHEN
Not this month.

She holds up a receipt. Sal squints at it. The bulb swings. Shadows slide across everyone.

MARISELA
Mr. Bianchini, I'd like to know where your nephew was Tuesday night.

SAL
Detective, he was here. Gutting cod. Ask the cod.

MARISELA
I'm asking you.

DOMINGO
(from the freezer)
Boss, she's wearing a wire.

Marisela and Sal both look at Gretchen. Gretchen blinks, then at Marisela. Domingo reaches for his pocket. Renzo lets go of the lid and it slams.

She goes for the door. He goes for her. Gretchen throws the receipts, a blizzard of paper. Marisela dives behind the crates. Sal pulls a gutting knife and Domingo draws a pistol.

RENZO
(shouting)
Nobody shoot! Nobody shoot!

A long frozen beat. Paper settles like snow.

DOMINGO
She's not wired. It's the bookkeeper.

GRETCHEN
(composed)
I don't wear wires, Mr. Tavares. I wear cardigans.

She peels the cardigan open. Nothing underneath but a blouse. Sal lowers the knife. Domingo lowers the gun.

SAL
Then who's talking to the police?

Everyone turns slowly to Renzo.

RENZO
Uncle Sal. I can explain.

SAL
(gently)
Of course you can, Renzo.

He puts the knife down. Picks up a mackerel instead. Weighs it.

SAL (CONT'D)
But first, you gut the fish.
